/// public.print_template_assignment — §5, WHICH design wins, and WHERE.
///
/// THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO isDefault COLUMN. Default-ness is the ROW'S
/// EXISTENCE. A pta_ row is the choice for one scope; templates that are merely
/// available are PrintTemplate rows matching the purpose, which is what the
/// "print in format" list shows.
///
/// That removes three defects at once:
///   * "exactly one default" becomes a plain unique index on the scope key,
///     with no flag anyone can forget to clear.
///   * THERE IS NO CLEAR STEP. Changing the default is an UPDATE of ONE row.
///     3.0's clear-then-set was scope-unaware, so on a mixed site setting the
///     Windows default silently unset the Linux one.
///   * A partial unique index on a boolean cannot be DEFERRED, so flipping two
///     rows in one UPDATE can fail on row order alone. With no boolean there is
///     no swap to get wrong.
///
/// Narrowest wins: counter -> branch -> company -> EVERY COMPANY, via
/// ptaSpecificity. Four rungs, not three: ptaCompanyId is nullable, so a
/// shipped design can be the default for every company that has not said
/// otherwise.
///
/// PAPER IS NOT IN THE KEY. Output mode is: a 3-inch thermal receipt genuinely
/// is a different artifact from an A4 tax invoice. Paper is a PROPERTY of the
/// chosen version, asserted against the counter's printer profile at render
/// time.
///
/// Declared here WITHOUT any CHECK constraint: ck_pta_output_mode,
/// ck_pta_device_needs_branch, ck_pta_branch_needs_company,
/// ck_pta_template_scope, ck_pta_copies and ck_pta_printer_one_of live in the
/// migration and in PrintTemplateAssignmentService.
///
/// DB-only objects Prisma cannot express, hand-authored in the migration:
///   ux_pta_scope     UNIQUE (COALESCE(company, nil), purpose,
///                    COALESCE(branch, nil), COALESCE(device, nil),
///                    output_mode) WHERE NOT deleted — "exactly one default
///                    per scope", as an index rather than as care. COALESCE to
///                    the nil uuid rather than NULLS NOT DISTINCT, because it
///                    is the same expression the resolver uses.
///   ix_pta_resolve   the render-time resolver, leading with the SAME COALESCE
///                    expression and ordered by specificity DESC, so that
///                    "this company's rows plus the global ones" is one index
///                    scan.
///   ix_pta_company / ix_pta_branch / ix_pta_device / ix_pta_purpose /
///   ix_pta_template / ix_pta_printer / ix_pta_created_by / ix_pta_modified_by
///                    FK-covering, all partial
model PrintTemplateAssignment {
  ptaId               String    @id(map: "pk_print_template_assignment") @default(dbgenerated("uuidv7()")) @db.Uuid @map("pta_id")
  /// NULL = EVERY COMPANY, the ladder's widest rung.
  ///
  /// A global row can only ever name a SHIPPED design, and that is enforced
  /// rather than trusted: ptaTemplateCompanyKey carries the template's owner
  /// and ck_pta_template_scope ties the two together. Without it a global
  /// assignment could point at one company's private design and every other
  /// company's till would render it — their logo, their address, on somebody
  /// else's paper.
  ptaCompanyId        String?   @db.Uuid                                     @map("pta_company_id")
  /// NULL = every branch.
  ptaBranchId         String?   @db.Uuid                                     @map("pta_branch_id")
  /// NULL = every counter.
  ptaDeviceId         String?   @db.Uuid                                     @map("pta_device_id")

  ptaPurposeId        String    @db.Uuid                                     @map("pta_purpose_id")
  ptaTemplateId       String    @db.Uuid                                     @map("pta_template_id")
  /// The OWNER of ptaTemplateId, carried so that the composite fk_pta_template
  /// can exist. Same idiom as the promotion schema's benefit lock: a fact a
  /// single-column key cannot check is made checkable by carrying the second
  /// half of the parent's identity.
  ///
  /// NOT NULL, and the nil uuid means "a shipped design" — matching
  /// ptlCompanyKey rather than ptlCompanyId, so no NULL ever reaches the key
  /// and MATCH SIMPLE cannot quietly stop checking. There is no default: the
  /// writer must state which design's owner this is, because defaulting it to
  /// the sentinel would mean defaulting to "shipped", and that is the one
  /// answer that must never be assumed. PrintTemplateAssignmentService reads it
  /// off the template rather than trusting the caller.
  ptaTemplateCompanyKey String  @db.Uuid                                     @map("pta_template_company_key")
  ptaOutputMode       String    @default("PRINT") @db.VarChar(15)            @map("pta_output_mode")

  /// Where the paper comes out for this scope. NULL = the server's default
  /// queue for the device.
  /// Points at public.printer_profile(prf_id) — §6.
  ptaPrinterId        String?   @db.Uuid                                     @map("pta_printer_id")

  /// The same answer given as a bare queue or share name, for a scope whose
  /// printer nobody has registered as a profile.
  ///
  /// A FALLBACK, never a copy of the profile's name: ck_pta_printer_one_of
  /// refuses it alongside ptaPrinterId. Two columns answering "which printer"
  /// is the same defect §5 spent its whole argument removing from 3.0's default
  /// handling, and a denormalised name on a MASTER goes stale the day the
  /// profile is renamed — unlike plgPrinterName, which is a snapshot on an
  /// immutable log and is meant to be frozen.
  ///
  /// What it costs: a registered profile carries the family, the codepage, the
  /// column count and what the device can physically do. A bare name carries
  /// none of that, so a render through this column falls back to the counter's
  /// defaults for all of it and prfPaperCode cannot assert anything — which is
  /// exactly the check that stops an A4 invoice going to an 80mm roll.
  ptaPrinterName      String?   @db.VarChar(150)                             @map("pta_printer_name")

  /// Overrides the purpose's copy count for this scope. NULL = use it.
  ptaCopies           Int?      @db.SmallInt                                 @map("pta_copies")

  /// GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED: 3 counter, 2 branch, 1 company, 0 every
  /// company. Read-only — writing it raises 428C9. The no-arg dbgenerated() is
  /// what stops `migrate dev` emitting DROP DEFAULT.
  ///
  /// A single CASE rather than added weights, because a counter row must also
  /// name its branch (ck_pta_device_needs_branch), so device 4 + branch 2 would
  /// score SIX and every reader would have to know that 6, not 4, means
  /// counter. Consecutive values cost nothing and cannot be misread.
  ///
  /// Rung 0 is its own rung and not shared with rung 1: if a global row and a
  /// company row both scored 0 the resolver would have a tie it cannot break,
  /// and ux_pta_scope would let both exist.
  ptaSpecificity      Int?      @default(dbgenerated()) @db.SmallInt         @map("pta_specificity")

  ptaRemarks          String?   @db.VarChar(250)                             @map("pta_remarks")
  ptaIsActive         Boolean   @default(true)                               @map("pta_is_active")
  ptaIsDeleted        Boolean   @default(false)                              @map("pta_is_deleted")
  ptaSyncDate         DateTime? @db.Timestamptz(6)                           @map("pta_sync_date")
  ptaCreatedOn        DateTime  @default(now()) @db.Timestamptz(6)           @map("pta_created_on")
  ptaCreatedBy        String?   @db.Uuid                                     @map("pta_created_by")
  ptaModifiedOn       DateTime? @db.Timestamptz(6)                           @map("pta_modified_on")
  ptaModifiedBy       String?   @db.Uuid                                     @map("pta_modified_by")

  // ── Relations ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  company             Company?      @relation("PrintTemplateAssignmentCompany",    fields: [ptaCompanyId],  references: [compId], onUpdate: Cascade, onDelete: Restrict, map: "fk_pta_company")
  branch              BranchMaster? @relation("PrintTemplateAssignmentBranch",     fields: [ptaBranchId],   references: [brId],   onUpdate: Cascade, onDelete: Restrict, map: "fk_pta_branch")
  device              DeviceMaster? @relation("PrintTemplateAssignmentDevice",     fields: [ptaDeviceId],   references: [devId],  onUpdate: Cascade, onDelete: Restrict, map: "fk_pta_device")
  purpose             PrintPurpose  @relation("PrintTemplateAssignmentPurpose",    fields: [ptaPurposeId],  references: [ppoId],  onUpdate: Cascade, onDelete: Restrict, map: "fk_pta_purpose")
  /// COMPOSITE, against ux_ptl_id_company_key — (ptl_id, ptl_company_key) —
  /// so that a company cannot assign another company's private template. Both
  /// columns are NOT NULL on this side and generated non-null on the other, so
  /// the key always fires; it subsumes the plain existence check a
  /// single-column key would give.
  template            PrintTemplate @relation("PrintTemplateAssignmentTemplate",   fields: [ptaTemplateId, ptaTemplateCompanyKey], references: [ptlId, ptlCompanyKey], onUpdate: Cascade, onDelete: Restrict, map: "fk_pta_template")
  printer             PrinterProfile? @relation("PrintTemplateAssignmentPrinter", fields: [ptaPrinterId],  references: [prfId],  onUpdate: Cascade, onDelete: Restrict, map: "fk_pta_printer")
  createdByUser       UserMaster?   @relation("PrintTemplateAssignmentCreatedBy",  fields: [ptaCreatedBy],  references: [usrId],  onUpdate: Cascade, onDelete: Restrict, map: "fk_pta_created_by")
  modifiedByUser      UserMaster?   @relation("PrintTemplateAssignmentModifiedBy", fields: [ptaModifiedBy], references: [usrId],  onUpdate: Cascade, onDelete: Restrict, map: "fk_pta_modified_by")

  @@map("print_template_assignment")
  @@schema("public")
}
